  "description": "A tiny lesson with a big payoff: Acetylcholine: The Movement Messenger.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Every voluntary muscle movement you make depends on one neurotransmitter: acetylcholine.",
      "visual": "💪"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Motor neurons release acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction to trigger muscle contraction.",
      "visual": "🏋️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "In the brain, acetylcholine plays a different role—it enhances attention and memory formation.",
      "visual": "🎓"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "The nerve agent sarin blocks acetylcholine breakdown, causing muscles to contract uncontrollably.",
      "visual": "☠️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Alzheimer's disease destroys acetylcholine-producing neurons, eroding memory from the inside.",
      "visual": "🧩"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "One molecule moves your muscles and sharpens your mind.",
      "visual": "⚡"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What does acetylcholine do at the neuromuscular junction?",
    "options": [
      "Triggers muscle contraction",
      "Inhibits muscle movement",
      "Produces myelin"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
